Phase‑1 (authoritative WebSocket + Node.js/TypeScript + Prisma/SQLite). I'll mentor you through architecture, tradeoffs, and a pragmatic, maintainable plan. I’ll explain why for each recommendation so you can reason about future changes.

High‑level architecture (Phase‑1, authoritative server)
Components
Browser client: canvas UI, pointer events → serialize stroke ops → send to server via WebSocket. Minimal app state: local undone stack for optimistic undo, but canonical state from server.
Authoritative WebSocket server (Node.js + TypeScript): single source of truth per room. Validates ops, applies them to room state, broadcasts to other clients.
Persistence layer (Prisma + SQLite): periodic snapshots of room state (JSON), lightweight metadata. Optional per‑op table if you want op-log later.
Minimal HTTP API (Express): create/list rooms, serve static client for dev, health checks, graceful shutdown endpoints.
Dev tooling: tests (vitest/jest), linter (ESLint), formatting (Prettier).
Why this shape
Simplicity and determinism: authoritative server reduces requirements on client logic and avoids complex merge logic initially.
Fast to implement and easy to reason about correctness for a developer new to real‑time systems.
Clear upgrade path: you can later add CRDTs/Yjs for offline-first and conflict-free merging.
Real‑time tech choice & main challenges
Choice: native WebSockets (ws package) + simple JSON messages
Why: native WebSocket dependency is minimal, broadly understood, and integrates cleanly with Node/TS. Using socket.io adds convenience but additional abstraction (and possible version coupling) that complicates learning the fundamentals.
Challenges and how to address them
Ordering & consistency
Problem: concurrent operations from different clients can conflict (two users drawing at same time is fine; two clients clearing or undoing needs ordering).
Phase‑1 handling: server applies ops in arrival order and broadcasts. If you need stronger causal guarantees later, migrate to CRDTs (Yjs) or attach causal metadata.
Practical fix: keep ops small (strokes with unique ids) and make destructive actions (clear) explicit with timestamps/sequence numbers so clients can reconcile.
Latency and UX
Problem: network delay leads to perceived lag.
Fix: optimistic local rendering — client draws locally immediately and sends op; server accepts and broadcasts. Clients must deduplicate by stroke id.
Reconnect/resync
Problem: client reconnects should receive canonical state.
Fix: on join send init_state snapshot (full state). Use periodic snapshotting on server for persistence; keep in-memory state for active rooms for low-latency.
Backpressure & message flood
Problem: pointermove events can generate many points.
Fix: client-side throttling/sampling, compress points (e.g., only send simplified strokes or every N ms), batch small ops.
Scalability and single process limits
Problem: a single Node process cannot hold all rooms for many users.
Fix: phase‑1: accept single‑process limits. For scale, move to multi‑process / stateless WS frontends with external state (CRDT replicas + shared persistence), or use sticky sessions + Redis pub/sub for room synchronization.
Security & malicious clients
Validate incoming messages (shape, sizes, rate). Limit stroke size and points per stroke. Rate limit per connection and per room.
Data serialization: JSON vs binary (Protobuf/msgpack)
JSON (recommended for Phase‑1)
Pros: human readable, no schema toolchain, immediate debugging in browser devtools and logs, fast iteration.
Cons: larger payloads (but typically fine for MVP), no enforced schema at runtime.
Why choose it now: prioritizes developer velocity and clarity. Use JSON and add lightweight runtime validation (zod/io-ts) to catch malformed messages.
Binary alternatives (Protobuf / MessagePack / CBOR)
Pros: smaller messages, schema evolution, faster parsing for large payloads.
Cons: tooling overhead, more complex debugging, learning curve.
When to adopt: when bandwidth or serialization latency becomes a real bottleneck (highly concurrent large rooms).
CRDT wire formats
If you adopt CRDT (Yjs), they have their own binary docs/encodings which are efficient and incremental. Transitioning then makes sense only once you need offline/merge semantics.
Initial data model sketch (practical, maintainable)
Design principles
Keep Room state as concise JSON snapshot of strokes (array of stroke ops). Each stroke is immutable and identified by id.
Persist snapshots, not every op, to reduce write amplification.
Keep per‑room in memory for active rooms; lazily load snapshot on first join.
Key entities (Prisma-friendly)
Room
id: String (room id)
stateJson: Json — full snapshot { strokes: [StrokeOp], lastModified: Date }
updatedAt: DateTime
(Optional) Stroke (for future op-log)
id: String (stroke id) @id
roomId: String
userId: String
strokeJson: Json
createdAt: DateTime
UserSession (ephemeral, in-memory)
socketId, userId, username, joinedAt, lastSeen, cursor
StrokeOp shape (example)
{ id: "uuid-v4", userId: "u123", username: "alice", stroke: { color: "#000", width: 3, points: [{x:12,y:34}, ...] }, createdAt: "2025-12-25T..." , meta: { tool: "pen" } }
RoomState (example)
{ roomId: "demo", strokes: [StrokeOp], lastModified: "2025-12-25T..." }
Why this shape
Immutable stroke ops are easy to reason about and deduplicate across clients.
JSON snapshot fits well in Prisma/SQLite for quick retrieval on reconnect.
Persistence strategy (safe and efficient)
In‑memory state for active rooms
Keep full RoomState in memory keyed by roomId. Fast reads/writes and broadcasts.
Snapshot persistence
Save the full room snapshot to DB:
Periodic saver: every 3–10s if room dirty.
On last-client disconnect: force a save.
On graceful shutdown: save all dirty rooms.
Why snapshots: fewer writes, simpler to implement. Choose interval based on acceptable data-loss window (5s means up to ~5s of ops lost on crash).
Optional op-log approach (phase‑1 -> phase‑2)
Keep per-op persistence (append-only stroke table) for full history and replay. Use snapshot + op-log technique to limit replay size.
Migration considerations
If room state grows unbounded, switch to chunked snapshots or store strokes in pages (e.g., store most recent N strokes in snapshot and older strokes in archive table).
Undo / redo design (authoritative server simple approach)
Approach
Client requests undo by sending undo with strokeId (or send undo request -> server removes stroke from live state).
Server checks permissions (only allow user to undo their own strokes, unless global undo is allowed).
Server updates in-memory state (mark stroke as removed or delete from array), broadcasts op_broadcast of type undo (with strokeId).
Persisted snapshot will reflect the removal on next save.
Why this approach
Simple and easy to reason about. Not conflict-free like CRDT-based collaborative undo, but acceptable for Phase‑1.
Caveat
Collaborative undo in CRDTs is complex. For now, keep undo scoped to the initiating user or provide global clear with timestamp.
Message protocol (simple, explicit)
Principles
Small set of clear message types, each with strict JSON schema validated server-side.
Every client message contains: type, roomId, userId (and optionally username), idempotency token if relevant.
Example messages
Client -> Server
{ type: "join_room", roomId: "demo", userId: "u1", username: "alice" }
{ type: "draw_op", op: StrokeOp }
{ type: "clear", userId: "u1", ts: "..." }
{ type: "undo", strokeId: "uuid" }
{ type: "presence", cursor: {x,y} }
Server -> Client
{ type: "init_state", state: RoomState }
{ type: "op_broadcast", op: StrokeOp, fromUserId: "u1" }
{ type: "undo_broadcast", strokeId: "uuid", byUserId: "u1" }
{ type: "user_joined", userId, username }
{ type: "user_left", userId }
{ type: "ack", messageId? }
{ type: "error", code, message }
Why explicit types
Easier to validate and test. Easier to extend with versioning later.
Concurrency, validation, and sanitization
Server-side validation
Use a small schema library (zod/io-ts) to validate message shape and bounds (max points per stroke, max bytes).
Reject malformed or oversized messages.
Rate limits and throttles
Implement per-connection and per-room rate limits (ops per second).
Drop or queue excessive messages.
Deduplication
Use stroke ids for deduplication. If server receives op with id already applied, ignore.
Scaling roadmap (how to get from Phase‑1 to production)
Short term (scale the MVP)
Horizontal WebSocket servers behind a load balancer with sticky sessions + Redis pub/sub to broadcast ops between processes (still authoritative but distributed).
Persist snapshots to scalable DB (Postgres).
Medium term (real-time correctness & offline)
Introduce CRDTs (Yjs) for per-room document replicated across servers. Use a central persistence for Yjs documents (y-leveldb, Postgres, etc.) and y-websocket for sync.
CRDT benefits: offline edits, merge-on-reconnect, conflict-free collaborative behavior.
Long term (multi-region, large teams)
Use sharding for rooms, event-sourcing + append-only op-logs, and materialized snapshots.
Add authentication, ACLs, and per-room quotas.
Observability, testing, reliability
Logging: structured logs (json), include roomId, userId, event types, error stack. Avoid logging full state indiscriminately.
Metrics: ops/sec, active rooms, connections, persistence saves, error rates, avg serialization size.
Tracing: instrument join and save operations for latency.
Tests:
Unit tests for store (load/save snapshot).
Integration tests that spin a WS server and simulate two clients exchanging ops.
Property tests for idempotency/deduplication.
Chaos: test reconnect scenarios and server restart to ensure resync is robust.
Security basics
Sanitize inputs to prevent oversized payloads and injection into logs.
Use TLS for production WebSockets (wss://).
Implement simple auth in Phase‑1: token or signed room invite links. At minimum, enforce username length and roomId patterns.
Avoid storing sensitive info in room snapshots.
Developer onboarding / tasks (practical prioritized checklist)
Day 0: scaffolding
Create server/ with package.json, tsconfig, basic Express app and ws server skeleton.
Add Prisma schema with Room model and generate client.
Day 1: in‑memory room management + WS protocol
Implement room map, join flow, init_state, op_broadcast, basic draw_op handling with validation.
Implement client-side minimal changes: connect WS, join, draw local strokes, send draw_op, render incoming op_broadcast.
Day 2: persistence and snapshotting
Implement store module (load/save via Prisma). Save snapshots every 5s if dirty, on last disconnect, and on shutdown.
Add unit tests for store.
Day 3: UX & robustness
Reconnect with exponential backoff, deduplication by id, client-side throttling for pointer events.
Implement undo/clear messages.
Day 4: polish & docs
Add README with run steps and testing instructions. Add ESLint/Prettier and basic CI script to run tests.
Acceptance criteria
Two browser windows in same room see each other’s strokes in real time.
Reloading a page after creating strokes recovers persisted strokes.
Server persists snapshots on interval and on graceful shutdown.
Unit tests for store pass.
Example Prisma model (simple)
Room model (Prisma schema snippet)
model Room { id String @id state Json updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt }
Optional Stroke model (op-log)
model Stroke { id String @id roomId String userId String stroke Json createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @@index([roomId]) }
Final notes — why this plan helps the learner
Stepwise complexity: you start with a clear authoritative model that’s easy to reason about and debug. You learn core real‑time patterns (socket lifecycle, validation, snapshotting) before adding CRDT complexity.
Explicit invariants: immutable stroke ops, unique ids, server-applied ordering make reasoning and tests straightforward.
Upgrade path: each choice (snapshot format, op ids, presence schema) is selected to be compatible with future CRDT or multi‑process designs.
Emphasis on maintainability: small modules (ws handling, store/persistence, room lifecycle) with tests and clear boundaries let a junior engineer iterate safely.
